WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.150
The tropical island of Bimini lies in the
northwest corner of the Bahamas off the coast

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.010
of Florida in the center of the island.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.550
The Worldview two satellite captures this
image.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:17.510
This is a really strange image because you
have this dark, green, lush landscape all

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:23.300
around, but you have this kind of like light
colored outline of what almost appears to be

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:23.990
a shark.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.090
Why is there a giant.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.170
Mound shaped.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.540
Like a big shark rising.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.130
Out of the swamp plant in the.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:37.470
Bahamas? The waters here are among the most
shark infested on the planet.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.410
There are at least 13 different species of
shark recorded here in teeming numbers drawn

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:50.250
to the islands from far and wide, from
cannibal hammerheads and aggressive, volatile

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.390
bull sharks to the lethal black tips and
tiger sharks.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.400
Among the largest and second only to the
Great white for recorded attacks on humans.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.310
The local Tiger Beach is one of the most
shark infested beaches in the world.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:14.340
We do know that the Taino people who
inhabited many of these islands in this area

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.370
would have been very familiar with sharks.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.940
Some individuals, if they were able to kill
or capture a shark, they would take the teeth

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.510
and make a necklace out of them as a way of
showing that they could conquer this animal

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.110
spirit.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:30.390
It's not strange that the natives who once
lived here should fear and respect the shark.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.000
The question is, did they worship it?

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.660
There's no question that ancient cultures
elevated or deified certain animals in their

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:46.200
cultures, creatures that they looked upon as
being powerful and and representing positive

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.510
or spiritual qualities that they admired.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:56.820
Ancient Egyptians worshipped certain animals
as gods, Horus, the Hawk, Anubis, the jackal

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.470
neck bed, the vulture.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.240
Their priests worshipped them with spells,
magic and sacrifice.

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:08.150
Did the ancient Taino people who for
centuries inhabited these islands do the

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.260
same? Most records of this.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:17.030
Primitive people were lost when the Spanish
conquistadors slaughtered and enslaved them.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.090
The only way to find out more is to examine
the site.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.040
Explorer George Coronas has carried out
research into great white sharks.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.590
He's come to the Bahamas to investigate the
image.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.750
But before he heads out to Bimini Island, he
wants to know what the locals make of it.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.910
He's been given a lead.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.150
Local amateur historian Ashley Saunders.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.250
I'm looking for.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.970
Ashley. I'm Ashley Sanderson.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.220
Oh, my name is George.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.170
I understand that you're the historian.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.220
I certainly am.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.840
I've been documenting the history of Bimini
now for better than 20 years.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.100
The Shark Mound.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.820
Is it manmade or is it a natural occurring
thing?

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.390
The shark mind has been shaped by humans.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.190
A human civilization.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.540
Prehistoric.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.980
Are there other sites in the area here in
Bimini that might suggest that there was an

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.670
ancient civilization that may have
worshipped the shark or may have lived here?

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:21.060
Well, in 15 feet of water, there's a
submerged set of stones that might be

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.910
pointing to ancient civilization.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.800
A prehistoric civilization.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.800
And. These stones have been baffling us on
this island for generations.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.580
Interesting. Is there anything near the Shark
Mound that may be of significance?

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.460
Well, it seems as if the nose is pointing
towards the spring, the healing hold, the

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.230
healing. The fountain of youth.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:48.900
Discovering the mythical fountain of youth
has long been the dream of explorers.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.320
Local legends say that it became the
obsession of Spanish conquistador Juan Ponce

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:54.920
de Leon.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.560
15 day ten.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:01.710
He came here looking for the fountain of
youth that he heard about from the Indians

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:02.850
down in Puerto Rico.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.600
So you think the healing hole may have been
the fountain of youth that Ponce de Leon was

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:07.290
looking for?

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:07.920
Might be.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:08.970
Interesting.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.770
Now George has a number of leads to follow.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:20.020
Bimini Shark Mound lies hidden in the
northwest corner of the island on the edge of

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.220
the coastal shelf.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.820
George must get there by boat.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:31.500
On his way, he stops to investigate what
locals believe to be an ancient underwater

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.220
road.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.800
We're anchored right above Bimini Road.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.660
It's a series of huge stone blocks that run
the entire length of the coastline.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.600
Some say that it's a remnant of a lost
ancient civilization that's now sunken under

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.990
the waves. I need to check it out for myself
and see what it looks like.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:56.800
If there are beneath the waves, the ruins of
some ancient construction, it would suggest

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.390
these ancient inhabitants were equally
capable of building a shark mount.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.630
But it's difficult to tell.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.700
At first glance, it's not really obvious what
is going on.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.050
See out of place.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:22.890
Bahamas is known for its sandy bottoms, but
here we've got hundreds and hundreds of these

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.760
huge stone blocks lined all the way along
the coast here.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.610
And we're talking about a series of what
appear to be manmade blocks laid out

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:39.420
relatively flat that extend for over half a
mile in a linear feature that absolutely

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.190
looks like it was constructed.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.950
But in fact, I think it is a natural
feature.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:50.040
And a lot of people say that Mother Nature
doesn't make 90 degree angles or straight

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.440
lines, and that's absolutely not true
geologically.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.720
We see this all the time.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.680
Either way, the undersea Bimini road looks
strange.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.040
If it's natural, then it's unusual.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.890
If it's manmade, then it's remarkable.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.260
George pushes on towards the large shark
shape indicated in the satellite image, which

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.160
seems to be perched on the coastal shelf at
the edge of the Bahamas.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:15.630
Yeah, this looks like.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:16.590
A turn out right there.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:22.430
But it's protected by a seemingly
impenetrable mangrove swamp.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:30.140
It's a forbidding place, swarming with
bloodsucking insects, mangrove thickets like

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:32.630
barbed wire. The water's teeming with
sharks.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:37.550
One thing to see something like this on a
satellite image is another thing entirely to

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.660
get to it on the ground. Obviously, it's not
so easy to get to this place.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:46.760
So if it is indeed manmade, it certainly was
not made any time recently.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.880
The water is shallow, but not too shallow for
sharks.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:53.450
You see a lot of sharks around here.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.190
Yeah. Yeah. What kind?

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:56.870
You got the lemon.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.610
You have the nurse sharks?

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:00.140
Yep. The reef sharks.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.480
The bull sharks. The tiger sharks.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.190
Oh, we have a variety.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.510
You see a baby shark?

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:07.760
Don't get excited either.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:09.860
If I see a baby shark, don't get excited.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.650
No. What if I see an adult shark?

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.150
Let it go faster.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.140
Just let him go fast. This lane is only wide
enough for either me or the shark.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.580
Open your leg and let him through.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:22.580
I'm not opening my legs for any shark.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:27.620
What is really strange is that the
environment is really inaccessible.

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:33.560
This whole swamp land is not a place where
you would normally find something like this.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.820
The mangroves are around the water, but
there's a clearing right here.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:45.620
You can sort of see if you look closely
where the mangroves basically stop.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:49.190
So shark mount has got to be very close now.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:51.620
I see.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:52.760
Dry land. Yep.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.790
After shark mouth.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:58.470
Bingo.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.910
George is immediately struck by the scale and
difficulty of this terrain.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:08.540
This labyrinth of mangroves.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:12.740
It's so confusing. I'm just trying to get my
bearings, comparing what I can see on the

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:15.500
ground with this old satellite image.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:17.030
And it's not easy.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.840
What I find interesting is building something
that you can't see must be really difficult.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:27.740
I mean, these guys can't look at the things
from above like we can today.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.240
Without a proper archaeological
investigation.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.150
It's hard to tell more about the shark
mountain.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.990
But there's one more lead for George to
follow.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:47.250
Could the healing hole described by locals
be the same fountain of youth described to

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.710
conquistadors in the 16th century?

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:54.880
And does the nose of the Shark Mound
indicate its location?

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:04.330
We believe that this is the nose of the shark
pointing this way, I think.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:11.740
So it looks like the body of the shark is
oriented more or less north, south, east,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.870
west. Pretty close, actually.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.120
It could be a coincidence. Or maybe someone
